== Bài 5B (231 từ – Chủ đề Unit 3: Music)
*Read the following passage and mark the letter A, B, C, or D to indicate the correct answer to each of the questions.*

#passage-box[
  Chau Van singing is an ancient form of Vietnamese performance art that harmoniously combines folk music, spiritual poetry, and graceful dance. Originating in the Red River Delta around the sixteenth century, this traditional genre has long been performed at sacred temples to worship national heroes and respected deities. In 2016, the cultural practice closely connected with Chau Van was officially recognized by UNESCO as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.

  In a classic Chau Van performance, traditional musical instruments play a vital role in guiding the audience's emotions. The central instrument is the *dan nguyet*, or moon-shaped lute, accompanied by bamboo clappers, small drums, gongs, and bronze cymbals. As the musical rhythm speeds up or slows down, skilled singers change #underline[#strong[their]] vocal tone to express joy, solemnity, or patriotic pride. The embroidered costumes are also vibrant and colourful, making every live show visually impressive from start to finish.

  Recently, young Vietnamese musicians have found creative ways to bring Chau Van closer to modern high school and university audiences. Several talented youth bands mix traditional lute melodies with acoustic guitar chords or lively electronic beats, #underline[#strong[praising]] historical heroes in a fresh, contemporary style. Short videos of these fusion performances have attracted millions of views and positive comments on social media. Thanks to such efforts, young listeners not only enjoy catchy rhythms, but they also feel proud of preserving Vietnam's rich musical heritage.
]

